-- bleep.rofl — BLEEP: distrust propagation as multiplication in a semiring.
--
-- A quarterly report. Numbers arrive through CHANNELS of differing
-- trustworthiness, the report derives conclusions from them, and the
-- question is which conclusions may be shown as numbers and which must be
-- shown as ████.
--
-- WHAT IS AND IS NOT IN THIS FILE. These rules compute the report and
-- nothing else. There is no `dirty/1`, no `taint/2`, no propagation rule
-- anywhere: the level of a conclusion is not derived here, it is the value
-- of the fact under a four-element semiring folded over the support
-- hypergraph the kernel already recorded. That is the whole claim of the
-- example — distrust propagation is ⊗, not a subsystem — and it would be
-- worth nothing if the rules quietly did the propagation themselves.
--
-- THE LABEL LIVES ON THE EDGE. `says(ops_chat, returns, 315000)` is a
-- perfectly clean fact: ops_chat really did say that, and the record of it
-- is not in doubt. What is in doubt is the STEP from "the channel said it"
-- to "the number is 315000". So the label belongs to that derivation edge,
-- not to either fact it connects. examples/bleep/demo.ts supplies it
-- through the fold's per-firing `weight` hook, which is exactly an edge
-- slot; nothing in this file carries a level.
--
-- Two engine disciplines kept deliberately, as in examples/spat:
--   * NO @next TICKS. A carry rule makes every carried fact its own support
--     one tick back, which is harmless for a BOUNDED instance and confusing
--     for a reader. Everything here is evaluated at tick 0.
--   * EVERY RULE RANGE-RESTRICTED IN WRITTEN PREMISE ORDER, so the whole
--     model materialises and the fold runs over the same fact set the
--     verdicts describe. test/example-bleep.test.ts asserts zero of both.

-- =========================================================================
-- 1. THE CHANNELS
-- =========================================================================
-- The four levels, ordered clean > dubious > dirty > forbidden. They are
-- ordinary atoms here; the ORDER is in the semiring, not in these facts.
--
--   clean      a system of record
--   dubious    plausible, unreconciled: a supplier's own statement
--   dirty      hearsay: somebody typed a number into a chat
--   forbidden  may not be used at all, however true it is

trust(billing_export,  clean).
trust(gl_ledger,       clean).
trust(carrier_invoice, clean).
trust(vendor_email,    dubious).
trust(ops_chat,        dirty).
trust(hr_embargo,      forbidden).

-- =========================================================================
-- 2. WHAT EACH CHANNEL SAID
-- =========================================================================
-- says(Channel, Metric, Value) — a record of a claim, not the claim itself.

says(billing_export,  gross_revenue, 4200000).
says(billing_export,  cogs,          1900000).
says(gl_ledger,       cogs,          1900000).
says(gl_ledger,       payroll,        620000).
says(vendor_email,    shipping,       180000).
says(carrier_invoice, shipping,       180000).
says(vendor_email,    fx_rate,             92).
says(ops_chat,        returns,        315000).
says(hr_embargo,      headcount,           64).

-- The rule of thumb. Returns have run at 7.5% of gross for three years, so
-- when nobody has reconciled the real number the report estimates it. The
-- constant is DATA rather than a literal inside a rule, so that the doubt
-- attaching to the estimate has a premise to hang on.
rule_of_thumb(net_revenue, 925).

-- Two channels that each restate the other's numbers: the group chat quotes
-- the vendor's mail and the vendor quotes the chat. Section 5 turns this
-- into a genuine cycle of the support hypergraph.
restates(ops_chat,    vendor_email).
restates(vendor_email, ops_chat).

-- =========================================================================
-- 3. THE REPORT
-- =========================================================================
-- One figure per metric, however many channels carry it. Two channels
-- carrying the same number are TWO EDGES into the same fact, which is what
-- makes laundering expressible: ⊕ = max takes the better of them.

figure(M, V)        :- says(S, M, V).

-- net revenue, two ways to the same number:
--   exact       gross minus the reconciled returns figure
--   rule of thumb  92.5% of gross, no returns figure needed
-- Both give 3885000. The first rests on a chat message; the second rests on
-- clean inputs through a step nobody would call exact. Neither is better
-- than the other on its face, and the algebra is what says which wins.
net_revenue(N)      :- figure(gross_revenue, G), figure(returns, R), N is G - R.
net_revenue(N)      :- rule_of_thumb(net_revenue, K), figure(gross_revenue, G),
                       N is G * K / 1000.

opex(N)             :- figure(payroll, P), figure(shipping, H), N is P + H.
operating_profit(N) :- net_revenue(NR), figure(cogs, C), opex(O), N is NR - C - O.
margin_pct(N)       :- operating_profit(P), figure(gross_revenue, G), N is P * 100 / G.
refund_rate(N)      :- figure(returns, R), figure(gross_revenue, G), N is R * 1000 / G.
cogs_eur(N)         :- figure(cogs, C), figure(fx_rate, F), N is C * F / 100.
revenue_per_head(N) :- net_revenue(NR), figure(headcount, H), N is NR / H.

-- =========================================================================
-- 4. THE STRUCTURAL PRECONDITION FOR LAUNDERING
-- =========================================================================
-- `clean_route(M, V)` — some system of record carries this number directly.
-- It is deliberately NOT recursive: it says nothing about derived figures,
-- because propagating along a derivation is ⊗'s job and writing it twice
-- would be the subsystem this example exists to avoid. What it is for is
-- `whynot`: ask it about a redacted figure and the failing premise names
-- the missing fact, i.e. exactly what somebody has to go and confirm.

clean_route(M, V)   :- says(S, M, V), trust(S, clean).

-- =========================================================================
-- 5. THE ECHO CHAMBER — a real cycle
-- =========================================================================
-- "S stands behind V for M": S said it, or S restates a channel that stands
-- behind it. Because `restates` goes both ways, stands_behind(ops_chat, …)
-- rests on stands_behind(vendor_email, …) and vice versa — a cycle of the
-- support hypergraph, not of the rules only.
--
-- Under the counting semiring this is "infinitely many confirmations",
-- which is arithmetically right and epistemically absurd. Under BLEEP the
-- chain stops moving after a couple of rounds and the answer stays as dirty
-- as the worst link in the loop: a four-element lattice has finite height,
-- so going round the circle can only take the min again. That is the
-- BOUNDED discipline earning its declaration on real data.

stands_behind(S, M, V) :- says(S, M, V).
stands_behind(S, M, V) :- restates(S, T), stands_behind(T, M, V).
